  "source": {
    "title": "Newry Reporter, 10 June 1920, p. 5 (\"Armagh County Council: Markethill Area\", with every count)",
    "publisher": "Newry Reporter (via the British Newspaper Archive)",
    "kind": "news",
    "check": "first preferences add to the printed valid poll; quota = valid / (seats + 1) + 1 as printed; each surplus equals the elected candidate's total less the quota and equals the printed transfers (plus papers with no further preference); each count is the previous count plus the transfers; every figure read from the page images"
  },
  "note": "Proportional representation (single transferable vote) under the Local Government (Ireland) Act 1919; polled Saturday 5 June 1920 and counted at Armagh by T. E. Reid, secretary of the county council and returning officer. Markethill electoral area (four seats): the electoral divisions of Aghory, Ballyshiel, Clady, Glenanne, Hamiltonsbawn, Hockley, Killeen, Lisnadill, Markethill, Mountnorris, Mullaghbrack, Mullaghglass, Mullahead, Poyntzpass, Richhill, Tanderagee Rural and Tullyhappy and the urban district of Tanderagee. Electorate 7,880; 5,478 votes polled, 332 spoiled, 5,146 valid; quota 1,030. Quinn's surplus of 2 was not transferred. Gibson was declared the fourth member at the sixth count without a quota. Sinton announced his withdrawal about a week before polling day but remained on the ballot paper; James McElroy withdrew his nomination. The Northern Whig (9 June 1920, p. 7) calls Hanna a \"Protestant Home Ruler\"; the Newry Reporter labels him N. and calls him the Nationalist candidate."
}
